 # Visualization and Rendering Techniques
-
-
 ## Overview
-This family covers methods for visualizing isosurfaces extracted from volumetric scalar fields, ranging from direct procedural ray-marching to explicit rasterization of pre-computed meshes. Unlike full volumetric rendering which accumulates light through the entire volume, these techniques isolate specific density thresholds to reveal internal structures or material boundaries [G]. The primary distinction among siblings lies in the representation: [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md) uses procedural ray marching for smooth, continuous appearance without explicit geometry, while [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md) and [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md) rely on explicit triangle meshes for standard rasterization. [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md) extends this by extracting and managing a subset of representative surfaces at different isovalue levels for interactive exploration.
+This family covers methods for visualizing isosurfaces extracted from volumetric scalar fields, ranging from direct procedural ray-marching to explicit rasterization of pre-computed meshes [G]. Unlike full volumetric rendering which accumulates light through the entire volume, these techniques isolate specific density thresholds to reveal internal structures or material boundaries [G]. The primary distinction among siblings lies in the representation: [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md) uses procedural ray marching for smooth, continuous appearance without explicit geometry, while [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md) and [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md) rely on explicit triangle meshes for standard rasterization. [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md) extends this by extracting and managing a subset of representative surfaces at different isovalue levels for interactive exploration.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid (Cartesian, octree, or tetrahedral) where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers [G]. For detailed traversal, surface determination, and mesh coherence mechanisms, see [Surface extraction](29-surface-extraction.md).
